Xpost Postscript Compliance
===========================

Where this interpreter departs from the published language, and nothing
else.

It used to list every operator, and two hundred and fifty-nine of those
lines said the operator's name and no more. A name with nothing beside it
carries no information a reader can act on: implemented is the expected
state, and the whole of what a reader needs is where it is not.

Such a list is also unable to report its own gaps. This one had eighty-
seven of them -- composefont, cshow, setbbox, the CIDInit and CMap
groups, globalvmstatus and the rest -- every one of them swept and tested
in the operator campaign, none of them ever written down here. Nothing
inside a list of what works can say what is missing from it.

So what is left is the exceptions: an operator that is partly there, one
that is not there at all, and one that is there with something worth
saying about how. What is NOT here is the assurance that everything else
is implemented, because a document cannot carry that assurance honestly.
tests/plrm-operators holds every operator the specification defines, and
a guard asks a running interpreter which of them it has -- so the
completeness question is answered by the interpreter and this file
answers the other one.

Notation
--------

 [ ] : not implemented
 [/] : partially implemented
 [X] : implemented, with the note beside it -- a qualification, not an
       assurance; an operator with nothing to say about it is absent
       from this file

Level 1 operators
-----------------

 [X] image (12-bit samples render at the raster's 8-bit depth)
 [X] setscreen (a spot procedure that leaves no result loses nothing
     from the caller's stack, where the reference interpreters error)
 [X] base font CharStrings (a face whose program cannot be read
     publishes glyph indices)

 [X] handleerror (an error out of a procedure-implemented operator
     restores the stacks to their depths at the call; operands the
     procedure had already consumed are not pushed back)

Level 2 operators
-----------------

 [X] setcolorspace (all colour-space families; colour rendering dictionaries
     are recorded, not applied)
 [X] setpagedevice (OutputDevice, PageSize, HWResolution, OutputFile,
     PageOffset, ImagingBBox; other keys reach the device as features.
     A device keeping its page as rows keeps only the rows an imaging
     bounding box reaches, so a mark painted outside the box a program
     declared is not rendered)
 [X] BeginPage, EndPage (called with the count of pages the device has
     transmitted, EndPage also with the reason -- 0 showpage, 1 copypage,
     2 the device going away -- and its boolean decides whether the page
     is transmitted. A handler that is not executable is refused by
     setpagedevice; one that answers with anything but a single boolean
     is a typecheck. A failing handler is let out of showpage and
     copypage, where a program can catch it, but not out of a device
     handover, which would leave the device impossible to replace.
     copypage ends a page with reason 1 and does not count it, as the
     operator descriptions have it; the reference interpreter collapses
     copypage into showpage, reason 0 and counted, as a LanguageLevel 3
     interpreter may)
 [X] image with dict argument (ImageTypes 1, 3 and 4; Interpolate
     smooths the samples of any shape on the direct raster path,
     while a mask still decides each pixel by its nearest sample;
     MultipleDataSources is held to the DataSource it describes, so a
     dictionary disagreeing with itself is refused)
 [X] setblackgeneration (recorded only)
 [X] setundercolorremoval (recorded only)
 [X] setcolorrendering (the dictionary is the conversion a CIE-based
     colour goes through: the MatrixPQR and TransformPQR white point
     adaptation, MatrixLMN, EncodeLMN, MatrixABC and EncodeABC, and a
     RenderTable of three or four components)
 [X] makepattern (every tile repaints its cell; text through a CMap
     composite under a pattern colour paints the underlying colour)
 [X] filter (decode filters: ASCIIHex, ASCII85, SubFile, RunLength,
     Flate, LZW, CCITTFax, DCT, ReusableStream; encode filters:
     ASCIIHex, ASCII85, Null, RunLength, Flate, LZW, CCITTFax, DCT.
     A data source or target may be a string, a file or a procedure,
     and a procedure is called as the filter wants data or needs to be
     rid of it)
 [X] defineresource, findresource, resourcestatus, resourceforall,
     undefineresource (an instance is held to the type its category takes
     and left read-only; resourceforall enumerates the instances in VM, not
     those present only as files on the search path)
 [X] resource discovery by filename (GenericResourceDir, GenericResourcePathSep,
     ResourceFileName; the directory is %null when the search path is empty)
 [X] idiom recognition (IdiomSet resource category, IdiomRecognition user
